1
00:00:06,214 --> 00:00:09,717
{\an8}Hey, it could do with a little
yard maintenance, don't you think?

2
00:00:10,593 --> 00:00:12,803
How does one person
live in a house this big?

3
00:00:13,262 --> 00:00:15,014
Let me see.
By being very rich.

4
00:00:15,473 --> 00:00:16,516
That's now what I meant.

5
00:00:16,641 --> 00:00:19,519
Ah, you mean morally.
Well, we've been called in

6
00:00:19,644 --> 00:00:22,146
so it's likely
that whoever lived here

7
00:00:22,355 --> 00:00:23,940
paid a hefty price
in the end.

8
00:00:28,528 --> 00:00:29,904
There is something off here.

9
00:00:30,530 --> 00:00:31,948
This place gives me the creeps.

10
00:00:33,199 --> 00:00:35,576
Normally, I'd be tempted
to mock your superstition

11
00:00:35,701 --> 00:00:37,912
but in this case,
I'm inclined to agree.

12
00:00:38,829 --> 00:00:39,872
Ladies first.

13
00:00:49,340 --> 00:00:51,926
Boss, this is Reggie Brooks
from private security.

14
00:00:52,426 --> 00:00:53,803
Found the body,
called it in.

15
00:00:54,095 --> 00:00:57,557
Hi. I'm Agent Lisbon with the CBI.
This is Patrick Jane.

16
00:00:58,224 --> 00:00:59,392
Hi. How you doing?

17
00:01:00,059 --> 00:01:01,102
Is this gonna take long?

18
00:01:01,227 --> 00:01:02,436
I'd really like to get out of here.

19
00:01:02,895 --> 00:01:05,773
Why don't you just walk us through
what happened this morning?

20
00:01:06,357 --> 00:01:09,026
Well, I was, doing my rounds
of the neighborhood

21
00:01:09,235 --> 00:01:10,403
this morning, as always

22
00:01:10,945 --> 00:01:12,780
when I got a call
that said, uh

23
00:01:12,905 --> 00:01:14,448
Ms. Vogelsong's doctor
wasn't getting an answer

24
00:01:14,574 --> 00:01:16,659
when he tried to call her
and asked me to follow up.

25
00:01:17,285 --> 00:01:18,953
Do you know
the name of the doctor?

26
00:01:19,829 --> 00:01:22,456
No, I don't.
you can call dispatch.

27
00:01:22,582 --> 00:01:25,751
Okay. So the doctor told you
to go and check on her.

28
00:01:27,670 --> 00:01:30,423
"I... I noticed the smell right away."

29
00:01:31,716 --> 00:01:33,968
"And I, followed it
to the library"

30
00:01:36,095 --> 00:01:37,305
"and... that's when
I called it in."

31
00:01:40,474 --> 00:01:44,186
The final remains of Elise Vogelsong
Sacramento's tragic heiress.

32
00:01:44,520 --> 00:01:45,563
You know the story, right?

33
00:01:45,771 --> 00:01:48,316
You know, orphan at 12,
widow at 18.

34
00:01:48,649 --> 00:01:50,443
Divorced 5 times
before she turned 40.

35
00:01:50,568 --> 00:01:52,778
My mother ate that stuff up
when I was a kid.

36
00:01:52,903 --> 00:01:54,238
Never heard of her.

37
00:01:54,447 --> 00:01:56,449
Check out this place.
What a creep show.

38
00:01:56,616 --> 00:01:57,950
And, look at this body.

39
00:01:58,075 --> 00:01:59,410
I mean, it's reduced
entirely to ash.

40
00:01:59,577 --> 00:02:01,329
You know, there've been a handful
of cases I've read about.

41
00:02:01,454 --> 00:02:02,496
I've never actually seen it.

42
00:02:02,663 --> 00:02:04,915
Spontaneous combustion,
they like to call it.

43
00:02:05,082 --> 00:02:06,125
It's in a book somewhere.

44
00:02:06,250 --> 00:02:07,835
"Bleak House." Dickens.

45
00:02:08,502 --> 00:02:11,714
Yeah, Mr. Krook, the rag and bone man
burst into flames in his parlor.

46
00:02:12,298 --> 00:02:14,050
It's not believable.
I didn't buy it, either.

47
00:02:14,258 --> 00:02:16,469
Oh, um, it's...
It's a misnomer.

48
00:02:17,303 --> 00:02:19,639
You know, it's not actually
spontaneous combustion at all.

49
00:02:19,930 --> 00:02:22,767
Uh, it almost always happens
with a heavy drinker and a smoker.

50
00:02:23,059 --> 00:02:24,935
They pass out with a cigarette
in their hand

51
00:02:25,061 --> 00:02:26,604
ember falls onto clothing,
starts to burn

52
00:02:26,729 --> 00:02:28,898
and smoldering cloth
acts like a wick

53
00:02:29,065 --> 00:02:30,900
and it turns the victim
into a human candle

54
00:02:31,901 --> 00:02:35,321
with the body's subcutaneous fat deposits
as a perfect form of tallow.

55
00:02:36,155 --> 00:02:37,281
A human candle?

56
00:02:38,074 --> 00:02:39,283
Isn't that awful?

57
00:02:40,034 --> 00:02:41,827
Yeah, what's produced is a slow
and all-consuming burn

58
00:02:41,952 --> 00:02:43,829
so in the end,
there's nothing left but ash.

59
00:02:44,664 --> 00:02:46,165
So this could be
an accidental death?

60
00:02:46,415 --> 00:02:47,875
Yeah. Very likely.

61
00:02:48,376 --> 00:02:51,420
You notice how weird the light is?
Kind of waxy and brown?

62
00:02:52,046 --> 00:02:55,591
That's because human fat is congealed
on all the light bulbs. I mean...

63
00:02:55,716 --> 00:02:59,220
All right. Don't say another word,
please, okay?

64
00:02:59,470 --> 00:03:00,930
This was murder

65
00:03:01,055 --> 00:03:04,517
which is ironic, considering
Ms. Vogelsong's appreciation

66
00:03:04,642 --> 00:03:06,227
and love of murder mysteries.

67
00:03:06,352 --> 00:03:07,812
- Murder?
- Yes.

68
00:03:07,937 --> 00:03:10,398
There's globules of glass
in the fireplace

69
00:03:10,648 --> 00:03:13,734
and, this scotch
was opened very recently.

70
00:03:13,859 --> 00:03:15,069
And that's a lot of scotch
for one woman.

71
00:03:15,277 --> 00:03:17,780
There were at some point
four glasses right here

72
00:03:17,905 --> 00:03:20,574
but now there are only three in the room.
Where is the fourth glass?

73
00:03:20,866 --> 00:03:22,952
So two people are sitting down
relaxing, having a drink

74
00:03:23,327 --> 00:03:24,412
now one of them is dead.

75
00:03:24,537 --> 00:03:26,455
The other one tried to conceal
his or her presence

76
00:03:26,789 --> 00:03:29,792
by throwing the fourth glass
into the fireplace. Murder.

77
00:03:30,209 --> 00:03:32,336
Oh, you think?
Who's the other person, huh?

78
00:03:32,545 --> 00:03:33,587
Why'd they kill her?

79
00:03:33,713 --> 00:03:35,381
Well, you know,
if you hadn't mentioned

80
00:03:35,589 --> 00:03:38,467
the congealed human fat
in the light bulbs

81
00:03:38,592 --> 00:03:41,721
I may have had the stomach
to stick around and find out but I-I...

82
00:03:42,263 --> 00:03:43,305
You're a ghoul.

83
00:04:07,077 --> 00:04:08,120
Oh, thanks.

84
00:04:15,252 --> 00:04:17,337
You know, my mom kept
a scrapbook of Elise Vogelsong.

85
00:04:19,715 --> 00:04:21,592
{\an8}You'd have thought she was family
the way she talked about her.

86
00:04:24,803 --> 00:04:25,846
Your mom like that?

87
00:04:26,889 --> 00:04:27,931
Nope.

88
00:04:28,056 --> 00:04:29,099
{\an8}I think for mom, it was a way

89
00:04:29,224 --> 00:04:31,268
{\an8}of feeling better about things
with my dad, you know?

90
00:04:32,060 --> 00:04:33,896
{\an8}No matter how bad
things got between them

91
00:04:34,772 --> 00:04:36,648
{\an8}it was always worse
for Elise Vogelsong.

92
00:04:38,484 --> 00:04:39,526
{\an8}I think that was it, anyway.

93
00:04:39,985 --> 00:04:41,737
{\an8}Have we got a name yet,
for the doctor who called?

94
00:04:42,529 --> 00:04:44,114
Dispatcher didn't write it down.

95
00:04:44,448 --> 00:04:45,866
He doesn't even know
if he got it or not.

96
00:04:46,408 --> 00:04:48,660
{\an8}Now this says someone poured
accelerant on Ms. Vogelsong

97
00:04:48,827 --> 00:04:49,870
and lit a match, right?

98
00:04:49,995 --> 00:04:51,455
{\an8}Right. Best arson teams in the world

99
00:04:51,580 --> 00:04:53,081
{\an8}would have missed it 99 times
out of 100.

100
00:04:53,499 --> 00:04:55,209
{\an8}If Jane hadn't been so sure,
I'd have missed it, too.

101
00:04:55,793 --> 00:04:58,170
{\an8}Spectrometer says traces
of ethyl alcohol and methanol.

102
00:04:58,462 --> 00:05:00,756
{\an8}I'm guessing Sterno as an accelerant.
Wouldn't take much.

103
00:05:01,340 --> 00:05:02,382
That's nice work.

104
00:05:02,549 --> 00:05:04,468
Yeah, next of kin didn't seem
to believe it, though.

105
00:05:05,344 --> 00:05:07,179
{\an8}Give it time. Lisbon and Jane
will convince them.

106
00:05:08,972 --> 00:05:11,391
{\an8}Can you think of anybody who may
have wanted to harm your aunt?

107
00:05:13,560 --> 00:05:14,728
{\an8}She was an old lady

108
00:05:14,853 --> 00:05:16,897
who devoted herself to charity
and helping veterans.

109
00:05:17,022 --> 00:05:18,065
{\an8}Who'd want to hurt her?

110
00:05:18,398 --> 00:05:20,275
{\an8}She was losing her mind
at the end.

111
00:05:20,400 --> 00:05:22,027
Have you taken that into account?

112
00:05:22,694 --> 00:05:24,655
{\an8}Losing her mind how?

113
00:05:27,032 --> 00:05:29,535
{\an8}All right, you know what my aunt
endured in her life?

114
00:05:29,868 --> 00:05:31,495
{\an8}The scandals, the tragedies?

115
00:05:31,787 --> 00:05:33,372
{\an8}Yes. I'm sorry.

116
00:05:33,747 --> 00:05:35,374
{\an8}Well, she was a strong woman,
despite it all

117
00:05:35,791 --> 00:05:38,085
{\an8}- until about two years ago.
- And what happened?

118
00:05:38,794 --> 00:05:42,089
{\an8}She stopped wanting to leave the house
started getting paranoid

119
00:05:42,548 --> 00:05:43,632
{\an8}thought people were out to get her.

120
00:05:44,132 --> 00:05:45,175
{\an8}Were they?

121
00:05:45,300 --> 00:05:47,553
{\an8}No. Not at all.
It was all in her head.

122
00:05:48,512 --> 00:05:49,680
{\an8}She fired all her servants

123
00:05:49,805 --> 00:05:51,974
{\an8}and was living in
that big old pile by herself.

124
00:05:52,766 --> 00:05:53,934
{\an8}Look, the police said

125
00:05:54,059 --> 00:05:55,686
{\an8}that there was nothing stolen
from the house.

126
00:05:56,061 --> 00:05:57,145
{\an8}There are no signs of robbery.

127
00:05:57,271 --> 00:05:58,772
{\an8}Someone commits a murder
in a house like that

128
00:05:58,897 --> 00:06:00,983
{\an8}and doesn't take anything?
No money, no jewels?

129
00:06:01,316 --> 00:06:02,526
No expensive works of art?

130
00:06:03,318 --> 00:06:06,071
- It doesn't make any sense, does it?
- Well, not yet.

131
00:06:07,030 --> 00:06:09,366
{\an8}It's an ongoing investigation.

132
00:06:09,533 --> 00:06:10,576
Yeah, it's ongoing.

133
00:06:10,701 --> 00:06:12,870
{\an8}Uh, could you provide us
with your aunt's doctor's name?

134
00:06:12,995 --> 00:06:14,204
{\an8}The one who called the security guard?

135
00:06:14,538 --> 00:06:16,248
{\an8}No, I didn't know her doctor.
I'm sorry.

136
00:06:16,623 --> 00:06:18,917
{\an8}As I said, my aunt had become
withdrawn in recent years.

137
00:06:19,209 --> 00:06:21,587
- I didn't communicate with her much.
- You had a falling out?

138
00:06:21,879 --> 00:06:27,175
{\an8}No. As her mind went, she became difficult.
I admit I distanced myself.

139
00:06:27,301 --> 00:06:28,886
I preferred to remember her
as she was.

140
00:06:29,219 --> 00:06:31,263
Okay. So before that,
you... you were close?

141
00:06:31,555 --> 00:06:33,181
{\an8}Well, some of my fondest memories
from childhood

142
00:06:33,307 --> 00:06:35,309
{\an8}were sailing with my aunt Elise
on the "Songbird."

143
00:06:36,310 --> 00:06:38,604
{\an8}Your aunt liked sailboats?

144
00:06:39,438 --> 00:06:41,356
Well, aside from the family's
charitable foundations

145
00:06:41,690 --> 00:06:42,733
it was her greatest joy.

146
00:06:42,858 --> 00:06:44,610
I don't think a week went by
that she wasn't out on the water.

147
00:06:44,902 --> 00:06:46,278
Even at her age?
Was that safe?

148
00:06:46,778 --> 00:06:49,781
{\an8}There was a girl went with her
down at the marina.

149
00:07:01,335 --> 00:07:02,377
Cayce Robbins?

150
00:07:06,256 --> 00:07:07,299
That's right.

151
00:07:10,010 --> 00:07:11,511
I'm Agent Lisbon
with the CBI.

152
00:07:11,637 --> 00:07:13,430
This is my associate,
Patrick Jane.

153
00:07:13,639 --> 00:07:14,932
We spoke on the phone.

154
00:07:15,307 --> 00:07:16,558
Oh, about Elise.

155
00:07:18,143 --> 00:07:19,186
You going somewhere?

156
00:07:19,853 --> 00:07:22,064
Yeah, Elise always said
she wanted her ashes

157
00:07:22,230 --> 00:07:24,191
scattered out at sea,
out of sight of land.

158
00:07:24,524 --> 00:07:26,818
So I figured it's my duty
to make that happen.

159
00:07:27,152 --> 00:07:28,278
I'm just waiting
to get the ashes.

160
00:07:28,612 --> 00:07:31,490
That's gonna have to wait
until after our investigation. I'm sorry.

161
00:07:31,865 --> 00:07:33,700
It's awful to think
what happened to her.

162
00:07:33,867 --> 00:07:35,911
Can you think of anybody
who may have wanted to kill her?

163
00:07:36,286 --> 00:07:38,830
I can't, but I only sailed with her.

164
00:07:39,289 --> 00:07:40,958
I don't know much
about her life on land.

165
00:07:41,375 --> 00:07:42,584
What about her mental state?

166
00:07:42,918 --> 00:07:45,170
In the last few years,
you see any signs of senility?

167
00:07:45,629 --> 00:07:49,216
Elise? No, she was sharper
than most people my age.

168
00:07:50,175 --> 00:07:51,927
I didn't like all the pills
she was taking, though.

169
00:07:52,135 --> 00:07:54,638
Doctors prescribe too many pills
these days, I think.

170
00:07:54,972 --> 00:07:56,473
What were the pills
she was taking?

171
00:07:56,640 --> 00:07:57,683
Or... or better still

172
00:07:58,100 --> 00:07:59,893
you know the name of the doctor
that prescribed them?

173
00:08:00,602 --> 00:08:03,230
You know, there's some in the cabin.
I can go get 'em.

174
00:08:03,647 --> 00:08:04,856
Thank you. Very helpful.

175
00:08:11,238 --> 00:08:13,573
Seems like a civilized way to travel,
don't you think?

176
00:08:13,699 --> 00:08:14,741
Are you kidding?

177
00:08:14,908 --> 00:08:16,284
Stuck on that thing
in the middle of the ocean?

178
00:08:16,410 --> 00:08:18,662
It's like being in a prison cell
with a chance of drowning.

179
00:08:29,131 --> 00:08:30,590
There you go.
Hope that helps.

180
00:08:30,924 --> 00:08:31,967
"Dr. Reinhardt".

181
00:08:32,801 --> 00:08:34,594
- Yeah, thank you.
- It does. Thank you.

182
00:08:35,303 --> 00:08:36,346
Yeah.

183
00:08:44,438 --> 00:08:45,689
Right this way, Agent Lisbon.

184
00:08:49,693 --> 00:08:50,736
Come on.

185
00:08:51,611 --> 00:08:53,572
I was just about to learn
how to drive her wild.

186
00:08:54,823 --> 00:08:56,116
Five easy steps.

187
00:08:57,451 --> 00:08:59,202
I've been Elise Vogelsong's
personal physician

188
00:08:59,327 --> 00:09:01,788
for almost two years.
I felt a particular concern.

189
00:09:02,789 --> 00:09:05,834
- She was in the early stages of Dementia.
- Why would you say that?

190
00:09:06,585 --> 00:09:08,253
Anyone who spent any time
with Elise Vogelsong

191
00:09:08,378 --> 00:09:11,089
would recognize the symptoms.
As a doctor, it was obvious.

192
00:09:11,339 --> 00:09:12,507
What kind of symptoms?

193
00:09:14,009 --> 00:09:16,720
Well, the onset of dementia
can manifest in various ways.

194
00:09:16,887 --> 00:09:19,931
In her case, it took the form
of an intense persecution mania.

195
00:09:20,223 --> 00:09:21,850
I was not comfortable
with her living alone

196
00:09:22,059 --> 00:09:25,395
but... Elise Vogelsong
was a very...

197
00:09:25,896 --> 00:09:27,481
Strong-willed woman.

198
00:09:28,106 --> 00:09:30,442
Is that why you were so quick
to send the security company

199
00:09:30,567 --> 00:09:31,610
to go and check on her?

200
00:09:32,235 --> 00:09:33,904
Well, I'd spoken to Elise
the evening before

201
00:09:34,071 --> 00:09:36,865
so when I couldn't get a reply
the next morning I became concerned.

202
00:09:37,449 --> 00:09:39,951
I worried she'd fallen
and hurt herself.

203
00:09:41,078 --> 00:09:43,038
The idea that she'd
been brutally murdered...

204
00:09:43,789 --> 00:09:44,998
it's beyond all comprehension.

205
00:09:45,415 --> 00:09:48,668
Well, better than the discovery
that her death was related to your care.

206
00:09:50,545 --> 00:09:51,755
That must have been a relief.

207
00:09:53,256 --> 00:09:54,299
Excuse me?

208
00:09:54,424 --> 00:09:56,635
Well, your primary emotion
as you talk right now

209
00:09:56,760 --> 00:09:58,512
about Elise Vogelsong
is that of relief.

210
00:09:59,846 --> 00:10:01,223
Why is that?

211
00:10:02,349 --> 00:10:05,185
Where do you...
Get this idea?

212
00:10:05,977 --> 00:10:09,356
Is this an effective method of investigation
making things up?

213
00:10:09,481 --> 00:10:11,608
Or do you consider evidence as well?

214
00:10:14,903 --> 00:10:16,029
Somebody told us they thought

215
00:10:16,154 --> 00:10:18,115
you were prescribing Elise
way too many drugs.

216
00:10:25,455 --> 00:10:28,416
This...
is a mild sleep aid.

217
00:10:29,543 --> 00:10:32,212
I was attempting to regulate
Elise Vogelsong's sleep cycles.

218
00:10:32,337 --> 00:10:35,090
Balanced sleep cycles
are a bulwark against Dementia.

219
00:10:35,257 --> 00:10:36,299
This is widely known.

220
00:10:37,092 --> 00:10:39,511
Now I understand you must pursue
all avenues of inquiry

221
00:10:39,678 --> 00:10:42,806
but embracing the medical
theories of laypersons...

222
00:10:44,099 --> 00:10:45,225
Does not seem productive.

223
00:10:45,350 --> 00:10:47,727
Did you approve
of Elise Vogelsong sailing?

224
00:10:48,687 --> 00:10:51,648
I did not. Not at her age
and her condition.

225
00:10:53,483 --> 00:10:56,153
But as I said,
she was a willful woman.

226
00:10:56,403 --> 00:10:58,280
You didn't think it was good for her
to get out of the house?

227
00:10:58,655 --> 00:11:00,615
Of course I did,
but not by going out to sea

228
00:11:00,782 --> 00:11:03,577
or spending time with mentally
unstable war veterans.

229
00:11:03,952 --> 00:11:05,120
That was her charity work?

230
00:11:05,537 --> 00:11:07,914
The Vogelsong soldiers' home.

231
00:11:08,498 --> 00:11:10,292
I told her it was dangerous, but...

232
00:11:13,378 --> 00:11:15,463
Curtis and Alex Wiley.
Both artists.

233
00:11:16,673 --> 00:11:20,385
They own the California
Impressionist Gallery.

234
00:11:21,386 --> 00:11:22,429
Successful?

235
00:11:22,554 --> 00:11:24,014
More enthusiastic than talented
from what I could gather.

236
00:11:24,347 --> 00:11:25,765
They're well-liked
in the community.

237
00:11:26,057 --> 00:11:28,185
So family money
keeps the business running?

238
00:11:28,894 --> 00:11:30,687
- Any trouble with the police?
- None.

239
00:11:31,897 --> 00:11:33,398
That's Cayce Robbins, 28.

240
00:11:33,607 --> 00:11:34,816
Originally from
New London, Connecticut.

241
00:11:35,150 --> 00:11:37,402
Been working on or around boats
at the Bodega Bay marina

242
00:11:37,527 --> 00:11:38,737
for the last six years.

243
00:11:38,945 --> 00:11:41,656
And the only thing in her file
is some unpaid parking tickets.

244
00:11:42,199 --> 00:11:44,492
- What about the doctor?
- Dr. Lance Reinhardt.

245
00:11:44,659 --> 00:11:46,870
Born in D.C., educated
in the U.S. and Europe.

246
00:11:47,037 --> 00:11:48,079
Impressive list of honorary degrees

247
00:11:48,246 --> 00:11:49,873
from prestigious universities
across the globe.

248
00:11:50,248 --> 00:11:51,625
Moved to California
about five years ago.

249
00:11:51,958 --> 00:11:53,084
Clean record in the state.

250
00:11:53,335 --> 00:11:55,170
See what else you can find out
about Reinhardt.

251
00:11:55,337 --> 00:11:57,297
See what he was doing
before he moved to California

252
00:11:57,422 --> 00:11:58,465
and keep me posted.

253
00:11:58,590 --> 00:12:01,301
I'm gonna go talk to the guy who runs
the Vogelsong soldiers' home.

254
00:12:05,513 --> 00:12:08,391
Elise Vogelsong was a wonderful,
wonderful woman.

255
00:12:08,516 --> 00:12:09,559
We've been praying for her

256
00:12:09,768 --> 00:12:12,604
and for whoever it was
that committed the horrible act.

257
00:12:13,480 --> 00:12:15,315
This place looks like
it has an interesting history.

258
00:12:15,690 --> 00:12:18,401
The Vogelsong fortune was made
by Elise's grandfather Ernst

259
00:12:18,610 --> 00:12:19,945
during the world wars.
Munitions.

260
00:12:20,487 --> 00:12:22,906
This foundation was created
to ease his conscience?

261
00:12:23,365 --> 00:12:24,407
Yes.

262
00:12:24,741 --> 00:12:26,117
And God bless him if it did.

263
00:12:26,785 --> 00:12:28,745
Elise says it gave
a purpose to her life

264
00:12:28,870 --> 00:12:31,206
a purpose that was missing
for many of her relations.

265
00:12:31,331 --> 00:12:32,374
Please, follow me.

266
00:12:40,465 --> 00:12:41,508
Chaplain Griffin

267
00:12:41,633 --> 00:12:44,219
do you know anybody who may
have wanted to do her harm

268
00:12:44,427 --> 00:12:46,388
- or had a grudge against her?
- Here? No.

269
00:12:46,680 --> 00:12:47,931
As I say,
she was a lifesaver.

270
00:12:48,139 --> 00:12:49,975
Last year, the... the heating unit
in the building went kaput

271
00:12:50,100 --> 00:12:52,269
she replaced the entire thing,
no questions asked.

272
00:12:52,560 --> 00:12:54,980
How was Elise Vogelsong's relationship
with the veterans?

273
00:12:55,188 --> 00:12:57,482
Oh, she was generous with
her time as well as her money.

274
00:12:57,691 --> 00:12:59,901
With a certain reserve, of course.

275
00:13:00,026 --> 00:13:03,071
But I did notice a bond forming between her
and one of the W.A.C.s.

276
00:13:03,196 --> 00:13:05,031
- Really? Which one?
- Lissie Calhoun.

277
00:13:05,282 --> 00:13:07,617
- She was a nurse in Vietnam.
- Do you think we can talk to her?

278
00:13:07,784 --> 00:13:10,370
Yeah. I mean, she's usually here
in the afternoons.

279
00:13:10,578 --> 00:13:11,621
I could tell her to contact you.

280
00:13:11,746 --> 00:13:13,456
- We'd appreciate that.
- Of course.

281
00:13:13,832 --> 00:13:15,292
I don't know what we'll do now
that Elise is gone.

282
00:13:15,458 --> 00:13:17,252
I mean, there's
no one to protect us.

283
00:13:17,502 --> 00:13:20,922
- To protect you from what?
- The rest of the family.

284
00:13:22,340 --> 00:13:24,926
The Vogelsong fortune breeds
great entitlement and great guilt

285
00:13:25,218 --> 00:13:26,261
Elise used to tell me.

286
00:13:26,386 --> 00:13:27,429
And according to her

287
00:13:27,554 --> 00:13:28,847
it had driven all of her
relations completely mad.

288
00:13:28,972 --> 00:13:30,348
All they did was fight
with each other over the money.

289
00:13:30,724 --> 00:13:32,892
Well, where does the money come from
that funds the soldiers' home?

290
00:13:33,184 --> 00:13:35,353
The Vogelsong Foundation
which Elise was in charge of.

291
00:13:35,603 --> 00:13:37,480
I mean, with her gone, I just...
I fear we might be shut down.

292
00:13:37,981 --> 00:13:39,024
Is that a real danger?

293
00:13:39,190 --> 00:13:41,109
Oh, yes. Sadly, yes.

294
00:13:41,234 --> 00:13:43,445
One of the relatives was
determined to destroy us

295
00:13:43,653 --> 00:13:44,696
and... and all of our work here.

296
00:13:44,946 --> 00:13:46,990
According to Elise,
it was an obsession of his

297
00:13:47,115 --> 00:13:49,159
to crush her and take everything
that was hers.

298
00:13:49,743 --> 00:13:53,496
- Do you know which relative?
- It was a nephew. His name is Curtis Wiley.

299
00:14:04,549 --> 00:14:06,301
Vogelsong legal disputes.

300
00:14:06,634 --> 00:14:07,677
Wanna help?

301
00:14:10,889 --> 00:14:12,223
- Rigsby.
- What?

302
00:14:13,683 --> 00:14:14,726
Somebody die?

303
00:14:15,518 --> 00:14:18,313
No. It's from Van Pelt.

304
00:14:19,230 --> 00:14:22,984
She's learning cross drive analysis
and anomaly detection.

305
00:14:24,778 --> 00:14:27,822
- And that makes you sad?
- I'm not sad.

306
00:14:28,656 --> 00:14:29,699
Yeah, okay, I'm sad.

307
00:14:30,283 --> 00:14:32,660
Every time she's been away in the past
and she sent a postcard

308
00:14:32,869 --> 00:14:35,747
she always addressed it to me
or, you know, Wayne and the gang.

309
00:14:36,748 --> 00:14:39,501
This is just addressed to "the bullpen"
like I'm nobody.

310
00:14:39,834 --> 00:14:41,586
Right. She's moved on.

311
00:14:42,462 --> 00:14:43,797
Yes, thank you. I know that.

312
00:14:44,172 --> 00:14:45,298
Like years ago.

313
00:14:46,299 --> 00:14:47,884
Well, years makes it
sound like decades.

314
00:14:48,009 --> 00:14:49,052
It hasn't been that long.

315
00:14:51,346 --> 00:14:52,555
You're losing your mind.

316
00:14:52,931 --> 00:14:54,724
How long has it been since you've
had relations with a woman?

317
00:14:58,186 --> 00:15:00,397
- Recently.
- Lying again. Find a woman.

318
00:15:04,943 --> 00:15:06,611
Yeah, you know what?
You're right.

319
00:15:07,404 --> 00:15:08,822
I will. It's what I have to do.

320
00:15:09,489 --> 00:15:12,450
"Bullpen."
Not even an "x-o" at the end.

321
00:15:13,743 --> 00:15:16,413
Move on, Wayne.
Move on.

322
00:15:29,882 --> 00:15:31,467
Hey, Cho,
what do you got?

323
00:15:33,803 --> 00:15:35,888
There's about 30 descendants
of Ernst Vogelsong

324
00:15:36,264 --> 00:15:38,516
and every one of them seems to be
contesting a part of the will

325
00:15:38,891 --> 00:15:41,310
or accusing the others of some
improper financial behavior.

326
00:15:41,477 --> 00:15:44,272
Did you find out what was going on
between Elise Vogelsong and her nephew?

327
00:15:44,439 --> 00:15:47,316
It was about a codicil giving Curtis Wiley
another chunk of inheritance

328
00:15:47,567 --> 00:15:49,026
and a seat on the foundation board.

329
00:15:49,402 --> 00:15:51,446
There's pages of claims
and counter claims

330
00:15:51,571 --> 00:15:53,948
centered on whether it took
effect at 35 or 40.

331
00:15:54,240 --> 00:15:55,283
Seriously?

332
00:15:56,075 --> 00:15:58,077
These people
are a law firm's dream.

333
00:15:58,202 --> 00:15:59,245
No kidding.

334
00:15:59,537 --> 00:16:02,874
And more recently, Curtis had been
making claims of managerial incompetence.

335
00:16:03,040 --> 00:16:04,792
Said that there were
accounting irregularities

336
00:16:04,959 --> 00:16:06,544
at the foundation
and the soldiers' home.

337
00:16:06,919 --> 00:16:09,213
Any idea the status of the case,
which way it was leaning?

338
00:16:09,589 --> 00:16:11,716
No, but I've got a call out
to the probate judge.

339
00:16:12,592 --> 00:16:13,634
And one thing's clear...

340
00:16:13,759 --> 00:16:15,928
this much paper means bad feelings
on both sides.

341
00:16:18,473 --> 00:16:19,515
{\an8}This is a family matter.

342
00:16:19,640 --> 00:16:21,684
{\an8}It's not something that outsiders
can understand.

343
00:16:22,101 --> 00:16:24,187
I understand
that you lied to us earlier.

344
00:16:24,395 --> 00:16:25,855
You and your aunt
hated each other.

345
00:16:25,980 --> 00:16:27,523
And you're greedy.
No, you're wrong!

346
00:16:32,570 --> 00:16:34,071
That's not true.

347
00:16:34,363 --> 00:16:36,449
I never felt any ill will
towards my aunt.

348
00:16:36,741 --> 00:16:38,409
I loved her.
I wanted to help her.

349
00:16:38,534 --> 00:16:39,577
By suing her?

350
00:16:39,785 --> 00:16:41,537
Interesting way
of showing your love.

351
00:16:42,038 --> 00:16:45,166
All right, look, from a financial point
of view something had to be done.

352
00:16:45,374 --> 00:16:47,668
My aunt was not making good decisions.
Her mind was going.

353
00:16:47,877 --> 00:16:49,879
Well, so you say, and Dr. Reinhardt

354
00:16:50,129 --> 00:16:52,215
but Elise's sailing companion
didn't say so.

355
00:16:52,548 --> 00:16:55,801
Excuse me if I don't put much trust
in what Cayce Robbins says.

356
00:16:56,260 --> 00:16:58,346
A reading of my aunt's will
showed that little thief

357
00:16:58,513 --> 00:17:00,848
manipulated my aunt
into giving her the "Songbird."

358
00:17:01,224 --> 00:17:03,226
Why shouldn't your aunt
give Cayce the boat

359
00:17:03,392 --> 00:17:04,477
if that's what she wanted?

360
00:17:04,685 --> 00:17:08,022
Because that boat is worth
a quarter of a million dollars!

361
00:17:08,147 --> 00:17:11,817
Look. All assets of any unmarried heirs
revert back to the family trust.

362
00:17:11,943 --> 00:17:13,903
That's how it's done,
as my aunt well knew.

363
00:17:14,111 --> 00:17:15,821
Apparently, that's not
what she wanted.

364
00:17:16,072 --> 00:17:17,281
Well, she was not
in her right mind

365
00:17:17,406 --> 00:17:19,116
and people were taking
advantage of that.

366
00:17:19,325 --> 00:17:21,911
The expenditures of the soldiers' home
were astronomical.

367
00:17:22,119 --> 00:17:23,162
One of the trustees told me.

368
00:17:23,287 --> 00:17:24,830
And now we're losing
the "Songbird"?

369
00:17:25,873 --> 00:17:27,959
Honey, this couple's interested
in one of your paintings.

370
00:17:28,251 --> 00:17:30,002
Do you think you could
come over and talk to them?

371
00:17:36,050 --> 00:17:37,802
My family's being robbed blind.

372
00:17:38,553 --> 00:17:40,304
It's my fiduciary responsibility to act.

373
00:17:42,598 --> 00:17:44,892
$250,000 is a lot of money.

374
00:17:45,017 --> 00:17:46,811
We should go talk
to Cayce Robbins again.

375
00:17:46,936 --> 00:17:48,646
I'm always happy
to take a trip to the ocean.

376
00:17:53,192 --> 00:17:54,735
Well, we could
get on board and wait.

377
00:17:55,152 --> 00:17:56,487
Not without a warrant, we're not.

378
00:17:58,030 --> 00:18:01,617
Rules are just so...
tiresome and boring.

379
00:18:01,951 --> 00:18:03,286
Ms. Robbins, hi.

380
00:18:03,411 --> 00:18:04,829
We're sorry
to bother you this late.

381
00:18:05,037 --> 00:18:06,581
No, please, call me Cayce.

382
00:18:06,706 --> 00:18:07,999
I hope I didn't make
you guys wait too long.

383
00:18:08,207 --> 00:18:09,792
I was just doing
a favor for a friend.

384
00:18:10,334 --> 00:18:12,753
We just had some questions.
We talked to Curtis Wiley.

385
00:18:12,962 --> 00:18:14,630
I bet he had a lot to say.

386
00:18:14,922 --> 00:18:17,842
- Yeah, he's very upset.
- He's very upset?

387
00:18:20,595 --> 00:18:22,555
Look, I-I didn't
expect anything

388
00:18:22,680 --> 00:18:25,933
and I'm honored that Elise even
wanted to give me "Songbird"

389
00:18:26,058 --> 00:18:29,353
but changing the will
affects the foundation.

390
00:18:29,854 --> 00:18:32,273
- What do you mean, changing the will?
- You don't know?

391
00:18:32,648 --> 00:18:34,275
I-I just got a call from the lawyers

392
00:18:34,442 --> 00:18:36,694
and the doctor said Elise
was non compos mentis.

393
00:18:37,320 --> 00:18:38,571
The will isn't valid.

394
00:18:38,738 --> 00:18:40,114
And that affects the foundation?

395
00:18:40,364 --> 00:18:42,575
Well, Elise left her seat
on the foundation board

396
00:18:42,783 --> 00:18:44,160
to the chaplain
of the soldiers' home.

397
00:18:44,577 --> 00:18:46,162
That goes, too,
with the will thrown out.

398
00:18:47,538 --> 00:18:48,581
That's interesting.

399
00:18:50,333 --> 00:18:51,375
Thank you for your time.

400
00:18:54,503 --> 00:18:56,172
Look, she wasn't crazy,
you know.

401
00:18:59,175 --> 00:19:01,427
It was just those pills
that doctor had her on.

402
00:19:01,552 --> 00:19:02,887
That's what made her like that.

403
00:19:03,137 --> 00:19:05,556
They were actually
just a mild sleeping aid.

404
00:19:05,973 --> 00:19:07,016
We checked.

405
00:19:07,475 --> 00:19:09,310
Well, there was more
than just those pills.

406
00:19:10,061 --> 00:19:11,103
A lot more.

407
00:19:12,563 --> 00:19:13,648
I thought you understood that.

408
00:19:24,158 --> 00:19:26,285
All right. What have you got for me
on Reinhardt?

409
00:19:26,410 --> 00:19:28,996
Well, it looks like Elise's sailing friend
might have had it right.

410
00:19:29,789 --> 00:19:30,831
Took some digging.

411
00:19:31,207 --> 00:19:33,334
Negligent homicide?
Where's this from?

412
00:19:33,584 --> 00:19:34,627
Monaco.

413
00:19:34,877 --> 00:19:36,587
You see why
the charge was brought?

414
00:19:36,837 --> 00:19:38,631
Overprescribing
a pain medication.

415
00:19:39,006 --> 00:19:40,049
Bingo.

416
00:19:40,174 --> 00:19:41,884
It was treated as a terrible
mistake at the time

417
00:19:42,009 --> 00:19:43,302
and the charges
were eventually dropped.

418
00:19:43,594 --> 00:19:45,805
Still, it's not something
that would inspire confidence

419
00:19:45,930 --> 00:19:46,972
in Reinhardt's patients.

420
00:19:47,139 --> 00:19:49,016
Yeah, which is probably why
a great deal of effort's

421
00:19:49,141 --> 00:19:50,184
been made to cover it all up.

422
00:19:50,351 --> 00:19:52,978
Uh, what about the records
for what he was prescribing for Elise?

423
00:19:53,229 --> 00:19:54,271
Trying to get a hold of them.

424
00:19:54,397 --> 00:19:55,690
We may need a warrant.
Talk to Reinhardt.

425
00:19:55,898 --> 00:19:57,441
See how he reacts
when you confront him.

426
00:19:57,608 --> 00:19:58,651
Sure. Should I take Cho?

427
00:19:58,776 --> 00:20:00,194
- I sent him to the soldiers' home.
- Okay.

428
00:20:15,084 --> 00:20:17,628
- You must be Agent Cho with the CBI?
- That's right.

429
00:20:18,087 --> 00:20:20,923
Like I told Agent Lisbon on the phone
I don't want to get anyone in trouble

430
00:20:21,048 --> 00:20:23,342
but I think those two have been
plotting this for some time.

431
00:20:23,551 --> 00:20:25,553
Wait. Slow down.
Who was plotting what?

432
00:20:25,803 --> 00:20:27,388
The nephew and that doctor.

433
00:20:27,847 --> 00:20:29,223
I mean, I didn't realize it
at the time

434
00:20:29,348 --> 00:20:30,891
but I think they've been
working together for a while.

435
00:20:31,100 --> 00:20:32,143
And what makes you
say that?

436
00:20:32,351 --> 00:20:34,311
Uh, about a week ago,
they came in to talk to Elise.

437
00:20:34,437 --> 00:20:36,814
They must have been talking about
the future of soldiers' home.

438
00:20:37,148 --> 00:20:40,067
- You sure about that?
- Okay. Here's what I saw...

439
00:20:41,026 --> 00:20:42,778
Elise was outside talking
with Lissie Calhoun

440
00:20:42,945 --> 00:20:44,780
when Wiley
and Reinhardt appeared.

441
00:20:44,905 --> 00:20:46,949
The... the conversation
got very heated.

442
00:20:47,241 --> 00:20:49,618
Now Lissie Calhoun...
she's the nurse, served in Vietnam?

443
00:20:49,869 --> 00:20:52,163
Yeah. I know Agent Lisbon
wanted to talk to her

444
00:20:52,288 --> 00:20:53,706
but she hasn't been in
for the last few days.

445
00:20:53,998 --> 00:20:55,082
Do you have her address?

446
00:20:55,541 --> 00:20:57,209
Yeah. I think I do.

447
00:20:58,002 --> 00:20:59,628
Follow me.

448
00:21:03,007 --> 00:21:05,259
Lisbon, I'm sorry

449
00:21:05,384 --> 00:21:07,344
but I think we might have to get back
to the crime scene.

450
00:21:07,678 --> 00:21:09,388
Really? That place was so creepy.

451
00:21:09,847 --> 00:21:11,515
Yes. But I-I'm worried

452
00:21:11,640 --> 00:21:13,184
we might have missed something
because of that.

453
00:21:13,350 --> 00:21:16,604
Okay, fine, but no jumping out and yelling
"boo" at me or anything like that.

454
00:21:17,313 --> 00:21:18,939
I mean, seriously,
what do you take me for?

455
00:21:19,148 --> 00:21:21,317
I am not going to answer that
because I'm a nice person.

456
00:21:26,906 --> 00:21:27,948
{\an8}"She paid through the month"

457
00:21:28,073 --> 00:21:29,700
{\an8}"but said she was going
to visit a friend."

458
00:21:29,909 --> 00:21:32,161
- Did she say where?
- No, she didn't.

459
00:21:32,578 --> 00:21:33,621
What'd she do this time?

460
00:21:33,871 --> 00:21:35,539
Told her any more of it,
I'd throw her out.

461
00:21:35,956 --> 00:21:38,292
Three strikes.
That's my rule.

462
00:21:38,501 --> 00:21:40,377
She hasn't done anything.
I just wanna talk to her.

463
00:21:43,088 --> 00:21:45,716
Well, don't get me wrong.
You know, Lissie's good people.

464
00:21:46,759 --> 00:21:50,346
But she's got impulse control issues
and problems with anger management.

465
00:21:51,472 --> 00:21:53,307
Think maybe she saw stuff
over in Vietnam

466
00:21:53,432 --> 00:21:55,100
that nobody should see,
you know?

467
00:21:56,352 --> 00:21:57,394
Yeah, I know.

468
00:22:03,150 --> 00:22:04,193
Plus she drinks.

469
00:22:04,610 --> 00:22:05,986
That's probably
where she is now.

470
00:22:08,197 --> 00:22:10,032
So you think the friend
she's visiting is made up?

471
00:22:11,116 --> 00:22:13,744
All I know is, I told her
she can't stay here if she's not sober.

472
00:22:14,286 --> 00:22:15,329
I can't put up with that.

473
00:22:16,705 --> 00:22:19,959
She may have been America's tragic heiress
but she sure knew how to throw a party.

474
00:22:21,043 --> 00:22:22,086
Look at these photos...

475
00:22:22,419 --> 00:22:26,257
hot and cold running servants,
fine food, champagne and wine.

476
00:22:27,424 --> 00:22:29,510
That must have been
something to see.

477
00:22:30,886 --> 00:22:31,971
She went crazy I guess?

478
00:22:47,903 --> 00:22:49,154
- Lisbon?
- What?

479
00:22:49,446 --> 00:22:50,906
Come take a look at this.

480
00:22:52,074 --> 00:22:54,034
Look at this shelf.
Tell me what you see.

481
00:22:54,243 --> 00:22:57,621
A picture frame, a silver candlestick
an old lace doily.

482
00:22:58,455 --> 00:22:59,582
One lace doily.

483
00:23:01,041 --> 00:23:02,376
The other doily's missing?

484
00:23:02,626 --> 00:23:04,378
Yes. Now look at this.

485
00:23:05,963 --> 00:23:09,008
On this shelf,
what is it...

486
00:23:10,467 --> 00:23:11,510
That you don't see?

487
00:23:13,637 --> 00:23:14,680
Well, that medal's gone.

488
00:23:15,806 --> 00:23:17,433
I knew we missed something.

489
00:23:18,475 --> 00:23:19,977
Okay, will do, boss.

490
00:23:24,982 --> 00:23:26,817
Jane thinks some kind
of small commemorative object

491
00:23:26,942 --> 00:23:28,694
was stolen
from the Vogelsong house.

492
00:23:29,028 --> 00:23:30,070
Huh?

493
00:23:31,739 --> 00:23:32,781
Never mind.

494
00:23:34,742 --> 00:23:36,577
So you're still on
that computer dating site, huh?

495
00:23:36,952 --> 00:23:38,370
Yeah.
It's really cool.

496
00:23:39,079 --> 00:23:40,664
You can customize the kind
of woman you wanna meet.

497
00:23:41,832 --> 00:23:43,667
Have you actually made
any dates

498
00:23:43,792 --> 00:23:45,669
or are you just hesitating
and second-guessing yourself

499
00:23:45,794 --> 00:23:46,837
like you usually do?

500
00:23:46,962 --> 00:23:49,798
Yeah, I've made dates...
several. So there.

501
00:23:49,924 --> 00:23:50,966
Glad to hear it.

502
00:23:52,343 --> 00:23:54,136
But maybe you can also help
around the office a little bit.

503
00:23:55,179 --> 00:23:56,221
Meaning?

504
00:23:56,430 --> 00:23:57,973
Isn't that the Reinhardt warrant
on your desk?

505
00:23:58,223 --> 00:23:59,934
No, I've been waiting
on the Reinhardt warrant for...

506
00:24:01,894 --> 00:24:02,937
Oh, crap!

507
00:24:15,447 --> 00:24:17,282
Dr. Reinhardt will see you
as soon as he can.

508
00:24:17,574 --> 00:24:20,661
You understand I'm an officer of the law
investigating a murder case?

509
00:24:20,827 --> 00:24:22,746
The doctor is with a patient
right now.

510
00:24:23,038 --> 00:24:24,623
But as soon as he's done...

511
00:24:26,708 --> 00:24:27,751
Hey!

512
00:24:29,169 --> 00:24:30,253
Get away from the machine.

513
00:24:30,837 --> 00:24:32,881
It's not what you think.
I didn't do anything.

514
00:24:36,093 --> 00:24:37,135
Phenobarbital...

515
00:24:37,844 --> 00:24:39,096
Clonazepam...

516
00:24:39,721 --> 00:24:40,973
Diazepam...

517
00:24:41,390 --> 00:24:42,516
Thorazine.

518
00:24:43,183 --> 00:24:44,518
Looks to me like you did quite a lot.

519
00:24:45,560 --> 00:24:47,479
Hands behind your back.
You're under arrest.

520
00:24:54,695 --> 00:24:56,154
Hey, I think I found
your missing object.

521
00:24:56,279 --> 00:24:57,489
Oh, yeah?

522
00:25:02,828 --> 00:25:04,454
It's called the Vogelsong Medal.

523
00:25:04,663 --> 00:25:05,789
And apparently,
it's kind of a big deal.

524
00:25:06,415 --> 00:25:08,166
That's from Channel 7 news
a few years ago.

525
00:25:09,167 --> 00:25:11,712
"Elise Vogelsong
welcomes foreign dignitaries"

526
00:25:11,837 --> 00:25:14,506
"to the annual
Vogelsong Foundation dinner"

527
00:25:14,840 --> 00:25:18,135
"wearing the renowned
and priceless Vogelsong Medal."

528
00:25:20,387 --> 00:25:22,806
Priceless, huh?
That explains why somebody stole it.

529
00:25:23,140 --> 00:25:25,517
Except that it's only priceless
when it's the Vogelsong medal.

530
00:25:25,892 --> 00:25:26,935
What does that mean?

531
00:25:27,102 --> 00:25:28,270
Well, melt it down,
it's worth almost nothing

532
00:25:28,395 --> 00:25:30,272
but keep it as the Vogelsong Medal
you can't sell it.

533
00:25:30,647 --> 00:25:33,442
So with all the treasures
in that house, why take that?

534
00:25:34,359 --> 00:25:35,402
Beats me.

535
00:25:36,528 --> 00:25:38,572
What about Dr. Reinhardt?
Did you talk to him?

536
00:25:38,739 --> 00:25:41,616
I'm about to. With the amount
that he was prescribing her

537
00:25:41,825 --> 00:25:43,577
it's no wonder people thought
she was losing her mind.

538
00:25:43,952 --> 00:25:45,495
I'm amazed
she could stay upright.

539
00:25:54,671 --> 00:25:56,590
Okay, I did not want
to prescribe her those pills.

540
00:25:56,798 --> 00:25:57,966
She made me do it.

541
00:25:58,508 --> 00:25:59,760
She blackmailed me.

542
00:26:00,093 --> 00:26:01,762
Okay, you can take a seat.

543
00:26:03,305 --> 00:26:06,141
So an old lady with rapidly
progressing dementia...

544
00:26:06,892 --> 00:26:08,977
you gonna say that she was
the bad guy in this situation?

545
00:26:10,353 --> 00:26:11,396
It's true.

546
00:26:12,397 --> 00:26:15,400
I don't know how she found out
about my past, but she did.

547
00:26:15,692 --> 00:26:18,070
And she said she'd tell everybody
unless I did what she said.

548
00:26:20,072 --> 00:26:22,783
I just want to help people.
I'm... I'm a good doctor.

549
00:26:23,533 --> 00:26:25,452
She would have ruined
my career.

550
00:26:26,203 --> 00:26:28,080
She was a cruel
and vindictive woman

551
00:26:28,205 --> 00:26:31,333
willing to do anything
just... to get her way.

552
00:26:32,334 --> 00:26:34,086
So you weren't conspiring
with Curtis Wiley

553
00:26:34,211 --> 00:26:35,545
to undo Elise Vogelsong's will?

554
00:26:36,505 --> 00:26:37,547
What? No.

555
00:26:37,756 --> 00:26:39,549
But you and Wiley did have
a conversation with Elise

556
00:26:39,716 --> 00:26:40,926
outside the soldiers' home,
did you not?

557
00:26:41,134 --> 00:26:42,803
Elise asked me
to meet her there.

558
00:26:42,928 --> 00:26:45,764
That was the first and only time
I ever met Curtis Wiley

559
00:26:45,889 --> 00:26:47,766
I swear on my mother's grave.

560
00:26:48,350 --> 00:26:51,061
I voided that will because
I believe Elise Vogelsong

561
00:26:51,269 --> 00:26:54,439
not to be in her right mind.
That's the only reason.

562
00:27:02,614 --> 00:27:04,074
I tracked down
Lissie Calhoun in Vegas.

563
00:27:04,241 --> 00:27:05,826
She used a credit card yesterday
to get a room.

564
00:27:05,992 --> 00:27:07,035
Should I send somebody out
to go get her?

565
00:27:07,202 --> 00:27:09,704
Pass it on to the locals.
Have them drop in see what she has to say.

566
00:27:10,122 --> 00:27:11,164
Will do.

567
00:27:11,665 --> 00:27:13,500
Oh, and I got some intel
on the lawsuit

568
00:27:13,667 --> 00:27:15,127
from one of the clerks
in the probate office.

569
00:27:15,252 --> 00:27:16,461
Why can't we get a judge
on the phone?

570
00:27:16,586 --> 00:27:18,130
Oh, he's stuck
in some mediation thing.

571
00:27:18,255 --> 00:27:19,631
Stay on the judge.
What did the clerk say?

572
00:27:19,798 --> 00:27:21,800
Basically, trusts and estates
law is pretty arcane stuff

573
00:27:21,925 --> 00:27:24,344
but the clerk was pretty certain
that Elise Vogelsong was gonna win.

574
00:27:24,511 --> 00:27:27,389
Okay. I wanna get a warrant

575
00:27:27,514 --> 00:27:30,308
to search Curtis Wiley's home
and place of business.

576
00:27:30,642 --> 00:27:32,352
- I wanna lean on him hard.
- You got it.

577
00:27:42,779 --> 00:27:43,822
Hello, agents.

578
00:27:43,947 --> 00:27:45,532
Uh, Curtis is painting out back.
Should I go get him?

579
00:27:45,657 --> 00:27:46,700
That won't be necessary.

580
00:27:46,825 --> 00:27:48,285
This is a warrant
to search your business.

581
00:27:48,410 --> 00:27:50,162
Could you step aside
from the desk, please?

582
00:27:50,328 --> 00:27:51,371
What?

583
00:27:51,496 --> 00:27:52,581
Step away from the desk, please.

584
00:27:54,040 --> 00:27:55,500
Curtis, the police are here.

585
00:27:55,709 --> 00:27:57,752
We've already searched your home.
This won't take long.

586
00:27:58,170 --> 00:28:00,839
- My husband will put a stop to this.
- No, he won't.

587
00:28:01,423 --> 00:28:02,632
What's going on here?

588
00:28:03,008 --> 00:28:05,010
This is a warrant that
was issued by a judge.

589
00:28:05,135 --> 00:28:07,762
It authorizes me to search
your business, your vehicle

590
00:28:07,888 --> 00:28:09,222
and your persons.

591
00:28:10,140 --> 00:28:12,142
I don't understand.
Why are you doing this?

592
00:28:12,851 --> 00:28:14,144
Where were you the night
of the murder?

593
00:28:15,270 --> 00:28:17,898
I was here at the gallery
going over paperwork.

594
00:28:18,398 --> 00:28:20,358
- Alone?
- No.

595
00:28:21,276 --> 00:28:23,945
No, I was here, too.
W-we were both here together.

596
00:28:24,946 --> 00:28:25,989
I think you're lying.

597
00:28:26,156 --> 00:28:28,033
"Hey, boss, I found something."

598
00:28:28,241 --> 00:28:30,327
Hey. Those are
my personal art supplies.

599
00:28:30,577 --> 00:28:32,120
There's... there's nothing
back there that you need.

600
00:28:32,245 --> 00:28:33,288
Really?

601
00:28:34,039 --> 00:28:35,248
This is an art supply?

602
00:28:36,499 --> 00:28:38,710
My great-grandfather's medal.
Where did you get that?

603
00:28:39,044 --> 00:28:41,588
Back there. It was wrapped in this.
Smells like Sterno.

604
00:28:42,047 --> 00:28:43,089
No.

605
00:28:43,215 --> 00:28:46,092
I'm arresting you for
the murder of Elise Vogelsong.

606
00:28:46,218 --> 00:28:47,260
What...

607
00:28:55,183 --> 00:28:57,227
Your client was locked
in a bitter legal dispute

608
00:28:57,352 --> 00:28:59,312
with the victim over
an extended period of time.

609
00:28:59,771 --> 00:29:01,773
Realizing that he had little
or no chance of winning the suit

610
00:29:01,940 --> 00:29:03,107
he set about trying
to get rid of her.

611
00:29:03,233 --> 00:29:04,901
Oh, this is ridiculous.
This...

612
00:29:07,237 --> 00:29:10,365
You found an ally in the form
of Dr. Lance Reinhardt

613
00:29:11,532 --> 00:29:13,910
and together, they started
drugging Elise Vogelsong

614
00:29:14,035 --> 00:29:15,912
in carefully calibrated doses
so that the world around her

615
00:29:16,037 --> 00:29:17,288
would believe that
she was going mad.

616
00:29:17,455 --> 00:29:19,457
I met the man once. Once.

617
00:29:19,832 --> 00:29:22,085
The murder would look to the world
like a terrible accident.

618
00:29:22,418 --> 00:29:25,421
Just another in a line
of tragedies that Elise Vogelsong

619
00:29:25,546 --> 00:29:28,466
had suffered in her life
and Curtis would inherit everything.

620
00:29:29,133 --> 00:29:31,469
This is slanderous.
What he's saying is slander, isn't it?

621
00:29:31,594 --> 00:29:32,804
Curtis, please.

622
00:29:33,680 --> 00:29:36,724
It's all very neat, but it is completely
circumstantial.

623
00:29:37,600 --> 00:29:40,311
You presented nothing but innuendo
and scurrilous gossip.

624
00:29:40,728 --> 00:29:42,772
Except for the fact
that your client

625
00:29:43,648 --> 00:29:45,566
couldn't resist taking
something from the victim...

626
00:29:46,818 --> 00:29:50,154
something that represented everything
she stood for as a Vogelsong.

627
00:29:55,994 --> 00:29:58,288
- The Vogelsong Medal.
- No...

628
00:30:00,373 --> 00:30:02,709
Which he wrapped in a doily
taken from the house.

629
00:30:04,168 --> 00:30:10,300
And that doily still has traces of the Sterno
he used to light his aunt on fire.

630
00:30:11,634 --> 00:30:14,679
Now... it's just a matter
of which one is quickest

631
00:30:14,804 --> 00:30:16,681
to make a deal
and testify against the other.

632
00:30:17,724 --> 00:30:20,852
I'm quite sure that Dr. Reinhardt
is signing a plea deal right now

633
00:30:21,102 --> 00:30:23,313
but if your client's clever enough
he might be able to save his own neck

634
00:30:23,438 --> 00:30:24,605
and just serve life without parole.

635
00:30:24,939 --> 00:30:25,982
Deal.

636
00:30:26,607 --> 00:30:28,526
I'm innocent. No.

637
00:30:28,735 --> 00:30:31,362
That was planted on me by you
for all I know. I'm innocent.

638
00:30:31,738 --> 00:30:32,780
Well, it's your choice.

639
00:30:35,825 --> 00:30:37,910
Don't move
and don't say a word.

640
00:30:40,496 --> 00:30:41,539
Agent, please.

641
00:30:44,083 --> 00:30:45,126
Give me a minute.

642
00:30:47,545 --> 00:30:49,213
I'm sure I can talk sense into him.

643
00:30:50,006 --> 00:30:53,468
- Don't take the deal off the table.
- Sure. Talk to your client.

644
00:31:06,773 --> 00:31:08,191
They're gonna turn on each other.

645
00:31:08,524 --> 00:31:11,736
It is gonna be an open and shut case
by the time we get it before a judge.

646
00:31:13,488 --> 00:31:14,530
It all fits.

647
00:31:15,156 --> 00:31:17,617
I'll give you that.
Yeah.

648
00:31:18,409 --> 00:31:20,870
Yeah. A psychological motive
for stealing the medal

649
00:31:20,995 --> 00:31:22,538
is particularly compelling.

650
00:31:24,040 --> 00:31:26,000
But there's something about it
that's not right.

651
00:31:26,417 --> 00:31:27,752
What do you mean?
We've got a killer.

652
00:31:27,877 --> 00:31:29,754
We've got motive,
and we got conclusive evidence.

653
00:31:29,879 --> 00:31:31,839
- What more do you need?
- I don't know yet.

654
00:31:32,090 --> 00:31:33,466
Oh, you're just being contrary.

655
00:31:33,633 --> 00:31:35,551
No, I-I swear I'm not.

656
00:31:36,177 --> 00:31:37,220
Hey, Cho.

657
00:31:37,345 --> 00:31:39,847
Hey, boss. Just talked to the probate judge.
He finally got in touch.

658
00:31:40,264 --> 00:31:41,307
Really? What'd he say?

659
00:31:41,432 --> 00:31:43,101
Well, he said that in discovery
widespread malfeasance

660
00:31:43,226 --> 00:31:44,852
was found in the running of the foundation.

661
00:31:45,103 --> 00:31:46,145
What does that mean?

662
00:31:46,270 --> 00:31:48,606
It means that Elise Vogelsong had run
through her entire inheritance

663
00:31:48,773 --> 00:31:50,650
and was plundering
the coffers of the soldiers' home

664
00:31:50,775 --> 00:31:51,818
to line her own pockets.

665
00:31:52,068 --> 00:31:54,404
In the past few months
she nearly emptied the bank accounts.

666
00:31:54,654 --> 00:31:55,905
Elise Vogelsong? The victim?

667
00:31:56,155 --> 00:31:58,408
The whole matter was gonna be referred
to the district attorney's office.

668
00:31:58,825 --> 00:32:02,161
Not only was the ruling going to be
a complete victory for Curtis Wiley

669
00:32:02,495 --> 00:32:04,080
but Elise Vogelsong
was going to jail.

670
00:32:04,330 --> 00:32:06,249
So you're telling me
that sweet old lady

671
00:32:06,374 --> 00:32:08,793
that was murdered
was really a liar and a cheat?

672
00:32:08,918 --> 00:32:10,253
Yeah, and soldiers' home
is bankrupt.

673
00:32:10,503 --> 00:32:13,881
And Curtis Wiley murdered his aunt
for a lawsuit he was gonna win?

674
00:32:14,173 --> 00:32:16,426
And if he waited a week
he would've gotten everything he wanted.

675
00:32:17,260 --> 00:32:18,469
That's horrible.

676
00:32:19,929 --> 00:32:22,181
And I was just gonna take her ashes
down to the boat.

677
00:32:22,390 --> 00:32:24,642
Oh, so that the girl could scatter
them at sea?

678
00:32:24,851 --> 00:32:26,102
That's very kind of you, Lisbon.

679
00:32:26,227 --> 00:32:27,270
Well, I'm not gonna do it now.

680
00:32:27,395 --> 00:32:29,522
I'm gonna have somebody call her.
She can come pick 'em up herself.

681
00:32:31,482 --> 00:32:33,025
Do you mind if I do it?

682
00:32:33,568 --> 00:32:37,572
I like the idea of giving
this girl some closure.

683
00:32:38,990 --> 00:32:41,159
Don't tell me you've gotten
sentimental all of a sudden.

684
00:32:41,284 --> 00:32:43,369
Not at all. I just think
it's the right thing to do.

685
00:32:43,744 --> 00:32:45,037
I'll call you when I'm done.

686
00:32:54,630 --> 00:32:55,673
Hey.

687
00:32:56,549 --> 00:32:59,886
- How's the, computer dating thing going?
- Great. Yeah. Great.

688
00:33:00,595 --> 00:33:02,346
I haven't met miss right yet,
but, you know

689
00:33:02,597 --> 00:33:06,267
I'm out there on the prowl,
just... moving on, like we said.

690
00:33:06,517 --> 00:33:09,854
That's funny.
There must be some mistake then.

691
00:33:10,438 --> 00:33:11,481
There's several women out there

692
00:33:11,606 --> 00:33:13,149
who say some guy's running
a scam online

693
00:33:13,649 --> 00:33:15,359
where he pretends to be
a handsome CBI cop

694
00:33:15,485 --> 00:33:16,986
named Wayne Rigsby
and makes dates with women

695
00:33:17,320 --> 00:33:20,072
and then he doesn't show up.
It's weird, huh?

696
00:33:23,284 --> 00:33:26,245
That is weird.
They're by the elevator?

697
00:33:26,537 --> 00:33:28,122
- Yeah.
- Okay.

698
00:33:46,891 --> 00:33:49,018
Hi, la... wow.
There's five of you.

699
00:33:49,810 --> 00:33:52,146
So I can explain.

700
00:33:57,985 --> 00:34:01,030
Wow. I'm... I mean,
I'm... I'm glad that they found

701
00:34:01,155 --> 00:34:02,782
who killed Elise.

702
00:34:03,574 --> 00:34:05,034
It's just...
It's all so sad, you know?

703
00:34:06,869 --> 00:34:08,746
Maybe now Elise
can rest in peace.

704
00:34:10,248 --> 00:34:11,290
Are those the ashes?

705
00:34:11,415 --> 00:34:15,086
Uh, yes. I, I remember you saying
you wanted to scatter them at sea

706
00:34:15,211 --> 00:34:17,797
so I brought 'em with me.

707
00:34:20,341 --> 00:34:21,384
Thank you.

708
00:34:21,509 --> 00:34:23,553
I really appreciate this.

709
00:34:24,345 --> 00:34:26,305
I know this is what
Elise would have wanted.

710
00:34:26,639 --> 00:34:27,682
Sure.

711
00:34:28,975 --> 00:34:30,142
Let's go, shall we?

712
00:34:32,603 --> 00:34:33,938
What are you doing?

713
00:34:34,272 --> 00:34:35,690
I always wanted
to take a boat trip

714
00:34:35,815 --> 00:34:38,401
and this is a particularly
nice boat, isn't it?

715
00:34:38,693 --> 00:34:40,152
You... you know, I don't think
it's a very good idea.

716
00:34:40,278 --> 00:34:42,572
You're very, inexperienced

717
00:34:42,697 --> 00:34:44,323
- and it can get very dangerous.
- Oh, no.

718
00:34:44,448 --> 00:34:45,491
It'll be absolutely fine.

719
00:34:45,783 --> 00:34:48,244
Uh, you got an emergency kit here.
That's good

720
00:34:48,369 --> 00:34:50,413
which reminds me,
I should take a little bit

721
00:34:50,538 --> 00:34:53,291
of the old motion sickness
medication

722
00:34:53,416 --> 00:34:54,458
just as a precaution.

723
00:34:54,584 --> 00:34:57,628
There we go, and one of these.
Marine flare, huh?

724
00:34:58,296 --> 00:34:59,338
Wow.

725
00:34:59,463 --> 00:35:00,965
- Okay. You know what?
- Cool.

726
00:35:01,090 --> 00:35:02,300
You ever had
to use one of these things?

727
00:35:03,092 --> 00:35:06,262
- Hey! What are you doing?
- Smoke! That's hot!

728
00:35:06,596 --> 00:35:07,638
Put that out!

729
00:35:07,763 --> 00:35:08,806
Calm down.

730
00:35:08,931 --> 00:35:09,974
Give me... oh, my God.

731
00:35:10,224 --> 00:35:11,267
Oh! sorry!

732
00:35:13,894 --> 00:35:16,480
Uh, mayday! Girl overboard!

733
00:35:17,148 --> 00:35:18,190
Get some help!

734
00:35:19,275 --> 00:35:20,860
Uh... help!

735
00:35:21,402 --> 00:35:22,445
Sorry! Sorry!

736
00:35:23,362 --> 00:35:24,405
Yep.

737
00:35:25,197 --> 00:35:27,533
Hello? I know you're down there!

738
00:35:30,286 --> 00:35:32,163
Elise Vogelsong, I presume.

739
00:35:33,122 --> 00:35:34,540
And that looks like
the money you stole

740
00:35:34,665 --> 00:35:37,001
from the soldiers' home.
Let's go!

741
00:35:38,252 --> 00:35:39,629
I'll get the one on the water.

742
00:35:42,757 --> 00:35:44,175
Who are you?
What are you doing on my boat?

743
00:35:44,300 --> 00:35:47,345
My name is Patrick Jane.
He's an agent with the CBI

744
00:35:47,470 --> 00:35:49,930
and you, my dear,
are under arrest.

745
00:35:53,517 --> 00:35:54,560
Bon voyage.

746
00:36:22,463 --> 00:36:24,840
I didn't know about the money.
I swear.

747
00:36:26,884 --> 00:36:28,928
And I didn't know she was
gonna hurt anybody.

748
00:36:29,303 --> 00:36:30,429
Somebody did get hurt.

749
00:36:30,971 --> 00:36:32,223
Lissie Calhoun died.

750
00:36:37,353 --> 00:36:39,146
She told me that her family

751
00:36:39,271 --> 00:36:41,357
was trying to get her
put away in a home.

752
00:36:42,441 --> 00:36:44,235
I was just trying
to protect her.

753
00:36:46,362 --> 00:36:47,613
I thought I was doing
the right thing.

754
00:36:48,322 --> 00:36:49,365
But you weren't Cayce

755
00:36:49,490 --> 00:36:50,533
and now you're an accessory
to murder.

756
00:36:50,741 --> 00:36:54,036
I'm not. I'm n... I'm not.
I-I can't be.

757
00:36:54,912 --> 00:36:57,081
I would never.
You have to believe me.

758
00:37:01,460 --> 00:37:03,087
You know, she was probably
planning to kill you, too?

759
00:37:07,508 --> 00:37:08,884
You were never getting
off that boat.

760
00:37:14,807 --> 00:37:16,058
I feel really stupid.

761
00:37:21,355 --> 00:37:22,398
What am I gonna do?

762
00:37:24,191 --> 00:37:25,234
Testify.

763
00:37:25,818 --> 00:37:26,944
We'll make a fair deal
for you

764
00:37:27,153 --> 00:37:28,404
but you have to play straight
with us, okay?

765
00:37:29,780 --> 00:37:30,823
I will.

766
00:37:32,241 --> 00:37:33,284
I promise.

767
00:37:35,786 --> 00:37:36,829
I believe you.

768
00:37:57,558 --> 00:37:59,018
So what if I took
the money?

769
00:37:59,560 --> 00:38:02,062
My grandfather made it.
It was mine by right.

770
00:38:03,481 --> 00:38:05,483
But Curtis wouldn't have
seen it that way, would he?

771
00:38:06,317 --> 00:38:08,736
Curtis?
Nosey little brat, that one.

772
00:38:09,528 --> 00:38:11,113
Ever since he was a child.

773
00:38:12,031 --> 00:38:13,991
And that's why
you contested the codicil.

774
00:38:14,867 --> 00:38:16,994
You didn't want Curtis
on the foundation board.

775
00:38:17,620 --> 00:38:19,121
Him, judging me?

776
00:38:19,538 --> 00:38:22,708
Telling me how to run my foundation
spend my money?

777
00:38:23,459 --> 00:38:26,045
Would've been
unendurable.

778
00:38:26,504 --> 00:38:27,546
So you hatched your plan.

779
00:38:27,880 --> 00:38:29,507
You found a doctor
you could blackmail

780
00:38:29,632 --> 00:38:30,674
to get the pills
you needed.

781
00:38:30,883 --> 00:38:32,676
Uh, was Cayce
in on the plan?

782
00:38:33,260 --> 00:38:36,847
I told her that Curtis and the rest of them
were plotting against me

783
00:38:37,306 --> 00:38:39,099
that it was the only way
I could escape.

784
00:38:40,643 --> 00:38:44,480
And she was already convinced
that I was the mother

785
00:38:44,688 --> 00:38:46,065
she never had.

786
00:38:48,067 --> 00:38:51,111
Girls like that
are so naive and foolish.

787
00:38:52,404 --> 00:38:54,156
And what about
Lissie Calhoun?

788
00:38:54,949 --> 00:38:57,076
Was she naive and foolish also?

789
00:38:58,953 --> 00:39:01,372
That was an unfortunate necessity.

790
00:39:03,457 --> 00:39:05,793
"But the woman was a tortured soul."

791
00:39:08,462 --> 00:39:10,881
"What I did was an act of mercy."

792
00:39:13,008 --> 00:39:14,051
"And, uh..."

793
00:39:14,260 --> 00:39:17,888
"I made sure that her death
was a peaceful one."

794
00:39:23,310 --> 00:39:24,645
You certainly did.

795
00:39:24,937 --> 00:39:27,398
Overdosing her on barbiturates
and burning her to ash...

796
00:39:27,523 --> 00:39:29,441
that is a peaceful way to go.

797
00:39:30,901 --> 00:39:33,487
Then all that was left was
sending Cayce to Las Vegas

798
00:39:33,654 --> 00:39:35,656
to rent a room with
the dead woman's credit card

799
00:39:36,198 --> 00:39:38,284
and planting evidence
on your nephew.

800
00:39:38,826 --> 00:39:39,869
Yeah.

801
00:39:41,453 --> 00:39:42,621
That was rather fun.

802
00:39:45,124 --> 00:39:48,586
Even if it did mean having to enter
that tacky little shop of theirs.

803
00:39:50,796 --> 00:39:54,216
Curtis had been eyeing grandfather's
medals since he was a boy.

804
00:40:04,810 --> 00:40:07,646
Thank you, Mr. Jane.
Thank you for saving my husband

805
00:40:08,564 --> 00:40:10,941
despite your colleague's
best efforts to imprison him.

806
00:40:17,823 --> 00:40:21,994
You can, thank me by forgiving
my colleague, Agent Lisbon

807
00:40:22,244 --> 00:40:24,413
and keeping the old soldier's home open.

808
00:40:24,538 --> 00:40:26,248
Oh, don't worry.
Soldiers' home will stay open

809
00:40:26,373 --> 00:40:27,917
as long as there are
veterans who need it.

810
00:40:29,460 --> 00:40:31,337
And we forgive you,
Agent Lisbon.

811
00:40:32,171 --> 00:40:33,213
Thanks.

812
00:40:34,173 --> 00:40:35,758
- Bye.
- Bye.

813
00:40:44,016 --> 00:40:45,059
What?

814
00:40:45,517 --> 00:40:46,685
We followed the evidence.

815
00:40:47,353 --> 00:40:51,732
You... you did.
You... followed the evidence.

816
00:40:53,901 --> 00:40:55,444
Okay. Okay, I was wrong.

817
00:40:56,528 --> 00:40:57,947
But we followed
the evidence.

818
00:40:58,614 --> 00:41:01,158
You know what gave me second thoughts
about Elise Vogelsong?

819
00:41:01,700 --> 00:41:03,452
She read too many
murder mystery novels.

820
00:41:03,953 --> 00:41:05,829
There you go.
That's not police work.

821
00:41:06,038 --> 00:41:07,081
That's guessing.

822
00:41:07,247 --> 00:41:08,749
You should try it sometime.

823
00:41:08,958 --> 00:41:10,417
Now I'm gonna pursue Red John.
Ciao.

